    53	
    54	## 5. Problems, ranked
    55	
    56	**1 → "Equivalent" is a margin effect, and three sources of error can change it.**
    57	Quote (abstract): "the registered procedure classifies R3 and C* as 'equivalent, direction R3 lower' within the registered margin of ±0.05".
    58	- **Margin.** It is 58 to 77 percent of R3's own gain (0.065 to 0.086). The estimated difference is a 32 percent relative increase (0.0856 / 0.0649).
    59	- **Search error.** The doubling check moves one cell by 0.054 (fresh3, C*, D2+H3; report line 225). That exceeds the margin.
    60	- **Markets.** A t-interval over the six market values gives [−0.013, 0.054] for D2+H3 (my computation, 5 degrees of freedom). It includes zero and crosses the margin. With the doubled-search values it gives [0.014, 0.045].
    61	- **Design.** The margin was set after development showed d of 0.027 and 0.038 (`predictions-v2.md`).
    62	- **Effect.** The paper's interval [0.020, 0.022] gives a false sense of precision. Neither "equivalent" nor "R3 lower" holds for the neighbourhood.
    63	- **Fix (analysis on existing records, plus text).** Report the market-level interval and the relative difference. Replace "equivalent" with "difference below the pre-set materiality margin".
    64	
    65	**2 → The two Q1 families are mostly one test.**
    66	Quote (p. 2): "pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within ±0.05 under both Q1 attack families".
    67	- X7-SC takes the D2+H3 winner as an incumbent (design 5.2). In 4 of 6 markets both the C* and R3 X7-SC cells have the same means as the D2+H3 cells (`stability-result.json`).
    68	- The X7-SC winners carry the embedding values (k_F = 1, a_F = 0, lambda = 1; report lines 208, 214, 220).
    69	- The paper does not say this. Prediction Q3-a is "fulfilled" with 11 of 14 cells at exactly zero for the same reason.
    70	- **Fix (text only).** State it. Report Q1 as one test with a partial second.
    71	
    72	**3 → The attack searches are too weak for "robust" or "equivalent".**
    73	- 21 of 98 doubling checks are flagged (`eligibility.json`).
    74	- A 7-knob family (X5-AG/v2) beats the 15-knob X7-SC against C* on fresh1, fresh2, and fresh3 (report line 166).
    75	- Searches select on 5 training seeds (`lottery_train.csv`, 5 rows per policy).
    76	- Fresh-market factorial cells have no doubling check, but Q2 reports intervals of width 0.001.
    77	- The paper mostly avoids "robust". Its Q2 "substitutes" claim and its Q1 label still depend on these searches.
    78	- My envelope check: using each rule's strongest family, d is 0.031 pooled and 0.070 on fresh1 (my computation from Table 15).
    79	- **Fix (needs compute).** Repeat the search until doubling passes on all Q1 and Q4 cells. Add the envelope d as an exploratory row (existing records).
    80	
    81	**4 → With no honest churn, age labels the attacker.**
    82	Quote (p. 7): "In a fixed population, a provider that keeps replacing can stay the newest identity, so R3's targeting of replacements persists beyond the window."
    83	- Nine honest providers never replace. "Newest" therefore always means the attacker.
    84	- Table 6 shows the cost: an honest replacement is audited at 92 to 93 percent after its window and earns about 4 percent less than under C* (2.74 against 2.86).
    85	- With honest entry, the attacker loses the "newest" position and honest newcomers pay the cost.
    86	- **Effect.** The G effect and the R3-against-C* result may not hold outside this model.
    87	- **Fix (needs compute).** Add an arm with an honest replacement rate.
    88	
    89	**5 → C* was not selected on attacker gain.**
    90	Quote (p. 6): "by the development tuning objective, which combines gross efficiency and attacker gain".
    91	- The objective is min((eff − 0.90)/0.90, (0.30 − gain)/0.30) (design 4.1). The first term is at most 0.111. The second binds only if gain exceeds 0.267.
    92	- C*'s score is 0.1032 (ledger 3.2). That is an efficiency of 0.993. Gain did not enter the choice (my reasoning).
    93	- **Effect.** Q1 compares on gain against a comparator chosen on efficiency. A gain-selected comparator can change the direction label.
    94	- **Fix (analysis on existing records).** Report the gains of all 12 configurations. If another has lower gain, run it on the fresh markets (needs compute).
    95	
    96	**6 → Corollary 1 is correct but close to a restatement, and the abstract drops its conditions.**
    97	Quote (abstract): "immediate free replacement makes the ban costless."
    98	- The corollary assumes that replacement restores the same state distribution. The zero continuation loss then follows at once.
    99	- The real content is the check for B2 and B3. That is one parenthesis and three proof lines.
   100	- Hidden conditions: honest rivals that do not react, re-entry with no lost round, no identity memory in B2's price posting (not checkable).
   101	- **Fix (text only).** State it as a remark with the conditions. Prove the B2 and B3 check from the rule definitions.
   102	
   103	**7 → Lemma 2 is correct but proves less than the text suggests.**
   104	- It counts audits. It says nothing about deterrence. The term "audit dues" suggests a charge.
   105	- The persistence remark is correct only while the identity is uniquely newest (see problem 4).
   106	- "Served newest first" uses "served" for audits; elsewhere it means allocation.
   107	- The design measures age from the first offer (design 3.2). The paper says "younger than L rounds". An identity that offers but wins no jobs can age out unaudited.
   108	- Theorem 1 is correct by my derivation. It is never given numbers and never used.
   109	- **Fix (text only).** Remove "audit dues", define age, move Theorem 1 to a supplement.
   110	
   111	**8 → Contribution (iii) is arithmetic, not a finding about R3.**
   112	Quote (p. 2): "an exploratory analysis of episode-level mixtures of public-family policies found substantially lower empirical gross efficiency than the registered W1 search".
   113	- A mixture of two policies has the weighted mean of their gains and efficiencies. I reproduced each row from two end points.
   114	- The W1 "attack" has gain exactly 0.0000 [0.0000, 0.0000], and the doubled winner has all knobs zero (report lines 175, 244). W1 returned the null policy. The label "supported" is degenerate.
   115	- **Fix (text only).** Say that W1 found no attack. Reduce the mixture to one sentence.
   116	
   117	**9 → The headline rule element is redundant, and the paper does not say so directly.**
   118	- P given G = 1 is −0.0014 (report line 32). G alone gives −0.177 of the total −0.178.
   119	- Amendment 3 added the priority phase after the predecessor was "killed". The second test does not support that addition. The title is "Checking Newcomers".
   120	- **Fix (text only).** State it in the abstract.
   121	
   122	**10 → The statistics do not address the main uncertainty.**
   123	- Every confirmatory p-value is at the resolution floor or equals 1 (`audit.json`).
   124	- The calibration multipliers (0.5 and 0.25) show that the bootstrap undercovers at 200 seeds on the fixtures.
   125	- Search error and market variation are outside every interval.
   126	- **Fix (text only).** Move the calibration detail to a supplement. Lead with the market-level variation.
   127	
   128	**11 → Process facts that the paper omits.**
   129	- The design says a fresh model session wrote H4 within 8 core-hours (design 5.3). An executor resumed that session and committed the bundle (ledger). The paper says only "written in a separate session".
   130	- H4 contains D2+H3 and ran on the development market only.
   131	- The prediction tally counts Q1-a-alt and Q1-d, which Q1-a decides. Q3-e passes by 0.000009.
   132	- **Fix (text only).**
   133	
   134	## 6. Novelty and related work
   135	
   136	The contribution is modest. Probation, credibility thresholds, and cheap-pseudonym costs are known, and the paper cites them fairly. The new parts are newest-first residual targeting under a hard audit quota and a registered comparison. The comparison is a null result.
   137	
   138	Missing, with my confidence:
   139	- **Harrington (1988)**, "Enforcement leverage when penalties are restricted", Journal of Public Economics 37(1). doi:10.1016/0047-2727(88)90003-5. It is the standard source for history-based audit groups with limited penalties. The paper cites its successors [15, 19]. I am confident of this citation.
   140	- **Dellarocas (2005)**, "Reputation mechanism design in online trading environments with pure moral hazard", Information Systems Research 16(2). doi:10.1287/isre.1050.0054. It treats identity changes. I am fairly sure of the DOI; verify it.
   141	- **Golle and Mironov (2001)**, "Uncheatable distributed computations", CT-RSA. doi:10.1007/3-540-45353-9_31. I am unsure of the DOI digits; verify it.
   142	
   143	I could not verify references [8], [14], and [24]. I do not know them.
   144	
   145	## 7. Presentation
   146	
   147	A reader gets lost in three places: the abstract (330 words, undefined labels), section 8 (calibration and block-hash detail before any result), and the prediction-eligibility text on pages 16 to 17.
   148	
   149	**Keep for a 12-page version:**
   150	- The model, the rules, and Corollary 1 as a remark.
   151	- One Q1 table with per-market values, frozen and doubled.
   152	- The Q2 table for one family.
   153	- The Q4 table.
   154	- Table 6 (the honest-replacement cost).
   155	- One welfare paragraph.
   156	
   157	**Move to a supplement:**
   158	- The first test, except three sentences.
   159	- The ulp deviation and Tables 19 and 20.
   160	- Tables 21 to 23 (predictions).
   161	- Tables 13 and 14 (lotteries), Table 12, coalitions, the repair block.
   162	- Theorem 1 and the calibration procedure.
   163	
   164	**Rewrite:** the abstract, to four findings in plain words. Give rules and attack families descriptive names, not codes.
   165	
   166	## 8. Decision
   167	
   168	| Venue | Score (1 to 5) | Acceptance probability | Confidence | Change that moves it most |
   169	|---|---|---|---|---|
   170	| WEIS 2027 | 2 | 20% | Medium | Add the honest-churn arm and present the paper as a negative result with the newcomer cost |
   171	| AAMAS main track | 2 | 10% | Medium | Search to convergence, so that the empirical-game claims hold |
   172	| ACM EC | 1 | 3% | High | A real theorem: a bound on gain under age-ranked audits with a quota, against history-based rules |
   173	
   174	**Next action:** do the analyses in problems 1 and 5 first. They use existing records, and they decide whether the Q1 label stays.

